  "text": "Mara meets people on boats. Grandma remembers whole evenings. I replaced a tile. I can tell you exactly how I fitted it, but I’m not sure that makes a story anyone would come to hear. I haven’t even written an ending.\n\nI looked once more at the ceiling. There was nothing moving there now, so there was nothing more for me to fix. I packed my tools, made sure I had left the damaged tile out of the way, and went back to my workshop.\n\n“I promised to have everyone’s bread ready today,” she said. “If those sacks get wet, I won’t be able to bake.” She looked from the flour to the roof, then back to the flour. I set down my tools.\n\nWe listened. Rain still pattered on the roof outside. Inside, the dripping had stopped. Rina bent over the sacks and checked the flour. It was still dry. She let her shoulders drop and stood up.\n\nI kept thinking I would bring a story when something worth telling happened. Most days I make something fit, or put it right. I thought you’d rather hear about something else.\n\nThe rain kept falling. You could hear it on the roof after I came down the ladder. But the spare tile covered the opening, so the water stayed outside.\n\nI’ve found the cracked tile. Rina has an intact spare on that shelf. I’ll do the work on the ladder. Pip, could you bring me the spare?\n\nThe spare is beside the opening, so the rain can still get through. I can lift that same tile and move it. We do not need another one.\n\nThe cracked tile is down here beside the ladder. The opening it left is still letting water in. Show me where the spare should go.\n\nThank you. I’ll take out the cracked tile first. Then direct me to put the spare over the opening so the rain stays outside.",
  "textSha256": "85bb27183b48ed86681ebb106cc358e4689cc61ad531407998494cd873dec90a",
